.TH DIFFALLA-SYNC 1 "November 2025" "diffalla 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
diffalla\-sync \- synchronize two directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diffalla sync
\fISOURCE\fR \fIDESTINATION\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Synchronizes directories using one of two modes:
.IP \(bu 2
Unidirectional: Makes DESTINATION match SOURCE (default)
.IP \(bu 2
Bidirectional: Merges changes from both sides
.PP
Features:
.IP \(bu 2
Conflict detection and resolution
.IP \(bu 2
Move detection (copy+delete → move)
.IP \(bu 2
Dry-run mode for safe preview
.IP \(bu 2
Progress reporting
.IP \(bu 2
Error recovery (partial sync on failures)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b, \-\-bidirectional
Enable bidirectional sync (merge changes from both sides)
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be done without making changes
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-progress
Show progress during sync
.TP
.B \-\-delete
Delete files in destination that don't exist in source (unidirectional only)
.TP
.B \-\-conflict\-resolution \fISTRATEGY\fR
Conflict resolution strategy (bidirectional only):
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B error
(default) - Abort on conflict
.IP \(bu 2
.B newest
- Keep file with most recent modification time
.IP \(bu 2
.B source\-wins
- Always prefer source
.IP \(bu 2
.B dest\-wins
- Always prefer destination
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Unidirectional sync (source → destination):
.PP
.RS
.nf
diffalla sync /source /destination
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bidirectional sync with conflict resolution:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diffalla sync /dir\-a /dir\-b \-\-bidirectional \\
  \-\-conflict\-resolution newest
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dry-run to preview changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diffalla sync /source /dest \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sync with deletion (mirror mode):
.PP
.RS
.nf
diffalla sync /source /dest \-\-delete
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sync with progress:
.PP
.RS
.nf
diffalla sync /large\-source /dest \-\-progress
.fi
.RE
.SH UNIDIRECTIONAL SYNC
.PP
Makes DESTINATION match SOURCE:
.IP 1. 3
Compares both directories
.IP 2. 3
Copies new/modified files from source to destination
.IP 3. 3
Optionally deletes extra files (with \-\-delete)
.IP 4. 3
Detects moves to optimize operations
.PP
Source directory is never modified.
.SH BIDIRECTIONAL SYNC
.PP
Merges changes from both sides:
.IP 1. 3
Detects changes since last sync
.IP 2. 3
Propagates unique changes to opposite side
.IP 3. 3
Detects conflicts (both sides modified)
.IP 4. 3
Resolves conflicts using specified strategy
.PP
Both directories may be modified.
.PP
Note: \-\-delete flag is incompatible with bidirectional sync.
.SH OUTPUT
.PP
Summary includes:
.IP \(bu 2
Files copied, deleted, moved
.IP \(bu 2
Bytes transferred
.IP \(bu 2
Duration
.IP \(bu 2
Conflicts resolved (bidirectional)
.IP \(bu 2
Errors/warnings
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (all operations completed)
.TP
.B 1
Partial success (some operations failed)
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (invalid flags, e.g., \-\-delete with \-\-bidirectional)
.TP
.B 3
File system error
.SH CONFLICTS
.PP
A conflict occurs in bidirectional sync when both sides modified the same file. Resolution strategies:
.PP
.B error
- Safe default, requires manual intervention
.PP
.B newest
- Automatic resolution, risk of data loss
.PP
.B source\-wins / dest\-wins
- Predictable, but always discards one side
.PP
Recommendation: Use \-\-dry\-run first to identify conflicts.
.SH PERFORMANCE
.PP
Optimizations:
.IP \(bu 2
Move detection reduces I/O
.IP \(bu 2
Parallel hashing (automatic)
.IP \(bu 2
Incremental sync (only changed files)
.PP
Typical performance: 10,000 files in <2 minutes on HDD
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diffalla (1),
.BR diffalla\-compare (1),
.BR rsync (1)
